.Dd November 22, 2026
.Dt NVIDIA 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvidia
.Nd NVIDIA GPU driver (kernel module nvidia.ko)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load the driver as a module at boot time, place the following lines in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nvidia_load="YES"
hw.nvidia.registry.EnableGpuFirmware="1"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver attaches to NVIDIA GPUs and creates
.Pa /dev/nvidiactl
and one
.Pa /dev/nvidia Ns Ar N
per GPU.
The attach, which happens when the module loads, only claims the device.
The GPU is initialised, and its GSP (GPU System Processor) firmware is
started, on the first open of
.Pa /dev/nvidia Ns Ar N ,
and it is shut down again when the last open closes.
.Pp
The module is native: it has no
.Dv MODULE_DEPEND
on the Linuxulator
.Pq Dv linux , linux64elf , linux_common
and imports no Linux symbol, so it loads and works on a host with no Linux
layer.
.Sh LINUX COMPATIBILITY
Linux binaries (the Linux
.Nm nvidia-smi ,
Linux CUDA, a Linux Vulkan driver) reach the driver through
.Xr nvidia_linux 4 ,
a separate module that belongs to the Linuxulator.
The core only announces its devices:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include "nvidia_bridge.h"

int  nvidia_bridge_register(const struct nvidia_bridge_ops *);
int  nvidia_bridge_unregister(const struct nvidia_bridge_ops *);
.Ed
.Pp
A provider registers a table of two functions,
.Fn nbo_cdev_add
and
.Fn nbo_cdev_del ,
with the interface version
.Dv NVIDIA_BRIDGE_KPI_VERSION
(1) and the size of the table.
The core refuses another version or size
.Pq Er EINVAL ,
and a second provider
.Pq Er EBUSY .
A core built with
.Ev NV_CORE_LINUX_COMPAT
carries the Linux bridge itself and refuses every provider
.Pq Er ENXIO .
On registration the provider is told about the devices that already exist.
The interface is versioned apart from the module: any change to the layout of
either structure raises
.Dv NVIDIA_BRIDGE_KPI_VERSION ,
and there is no compatible extension.
.Pp
Because
.Xr nvidia_linux 4
depends on
.Nm ,
.Nm
cannot be unloaded while the provider is loaded:
.Xr kldunload 8
fails with
.Er EBUSY
and the module stays; unload the provider first.
.Pp
To build the old in-core Linux bridge, which makes
.Nm
depend on the Linuxulator again, build with
.Ev NV_CORE_LINUX_COMPAT Ns = Ns Cm yes .
.Sh FIRMWARE
The GPU runs on GSP firmware, which the driver gets from
.Xr firmware 9 .
The images live in firmware modules.
.Nm nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw
serves Ampere (other than GA100), Ada, Hopper and Blackwell GPUs on every
architecture.
.Nm nvidia_gsp_tu10x_fw
serves Turing and GA100 GPUs and exists on amd64 only; the arm64 build does not
support those GPUs.
.Pp
.Nm
depends on the modules built for its architecture, so
.Xr kldload 8
and the loader load them with the driver, before the driver's own load
handler runs, provided
.Xr kldxref 8
has indexed the directory they are installed in.
The image is then already registered with
.Xr firmware 9
when the GPU is first opened, which lets an unprivileged process make that
first open: loading a firmware module needs the privilege
.Dv PRIV_FIRMWARE_LOAD ,
which an opening process need not have.
.Pp
The kernel names each module after its file with
.Dq _fw
appended, for example
.Dq nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw_fw
in
.Xr kldstat 8 .
A firmware module cannot be unloaded while
.Nm
is loaded.
When the driver is loaded from a build directory by path, load the firmware
module by path first: a dependency that is already loaded is satisfied.
.Sh INSTALLATION
On a stock
.Fx
arm64 install, from a checkout of the repository, as a normal user who can
become root with
doas or sudo from ports:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
make install-prereqs YES=1 ACCEPT_NVIDIA_LICENSE=1
make install YES=1
make configure YES=1
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm install-prereqs
installs the packages the build needs, fetches NVIDIA's driver tarball and
open-gpu-kernel-modules, the
.Fx
source of the running kernel and drm-kmod.
The GSP firmware comes from NVIDIA's tarball; it is fetched on the machine,
not shipped.
NVIDIA's terms ask everyone who installs the driver to read and accept its
licence first, so the install never accepts it on its own:
.Va YES=1
alone does not, and stops with that explanation.
.Va ACCEPT_NVIDIA_LICENSE=1
accepts NVIDIA's driver licence;
.Va ACCEPT_ALL_LICENSES=1
accepts every licence the install meets.
Without either, on a terminal, the licence is shown and the user is asked.
.Pp
.Cm install
installs the modules in
.Pa /boot/modules
and runs
.Xr kldxref 8 .
.Cm configure
sets
.Va hw.nvidia.registry.EnableGpuFirmware
in
.Xr loader.conf 5
and
.Va nvidia_gb10_autoload
in
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
which the installed
.Xr devd 8
rule reads before it loads
.Nm nvidia-drm
when an NVIDIA display device attaches to the
.Xr pci 4
bus.
.Cm deinstall
removes everything
.Cm install
and
.Cm configure
wrote.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
All variables in this section are read-only.
.Pp
.Va hw.nvidia.version
is the full NVRM banner of the loaded
.Nm nvidia
module.
The following variables are derived from it:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_string
The version token selected from the banner, for example
.Qq 580.178.04 ,
or an empty string when no version can be parsed.
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_major
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_minor
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_patch
The corresponding integer version parts.
A part that the version does not have reads 0:
.Qq 580.178
gives
.Va hw.nvidia.version_patch
0.
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_available
1 when the banner version was parsed, otherwise 0.
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_suffix
The version suffix, including its leading delimiter, or an empty string.
.El
.Pp
.Va hw.nvidia.device_count
is the number of NVIDIA GPUs the driver has attached.
The variable exists for as long as the module is loaded, so a host with no
NVIDIA GPU reads 0 instead of finding no variable at all.
The count is the number of devices attached when the variable is read.
A GPU that appears after the module was loaded is not counted: the driver
does not attach a GPU that turns up later.
.Pp
Each GPU has the strings
.Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .vbios
and
.Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .firmware .
The following integer and suffix variables are parsed from those two strings:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .vbios_major
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .vbios_minor
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .vbios_patch
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .vbios_build
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .vbios_revision
The five VBIOS version fields.
The VBIOS string is hexadecimal; these variables show each field in decimal,
so the VBIOS
.Qq 96.06.1e.00.4a
reads 150, 6, 30, 0 and 74.
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .vbios_available
1 when the VBIOS version was parsed, otherwise 0.
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .vbios_suffix
The VBIOS version suffix, or an empty string.
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .firmware_major
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .firmware_minor
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .firmware_patch
The firmware version parts, parsed as decimal integers.
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .firmware_available
1 when the firmware version was parsed, otherwise 0.
.It Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .firmware_suffix
The firmware version suffix, or an empty string.
.El
.Pp
Before the GPU is initialized, the VBIOS string can be
.Qq ??.??.??.??.??
and the firmware string
.Qq N/A .
The availability variable then reads 0 and every part reads 0.
An absent suffix is an empty string.
.Pp
Each read of any of these variables is fetched from the resource manager anew
and parsed on its own; no two variables share one fetch.
The GPU can become ready between two reads, so a part read first and its
availability variable read second can disagree.
A reader that needs a consistent set reads the string variable
.Pq Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .vbios
or
.Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N Ns .firmware )
once and parses it, or reads the availability variable, then the parts,
then reads it again after the parts and repeats while it changed.
.Pp
Two variables identify the GPU.
.Ar N
is the device unit number: it is the same unit number as in
.Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N ,
so
.Va dev.nvidia.\& Ns Ar N
and
.Va hw.nvidia.gpus.\& Ns Ar N
describe one GPU.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va dev.nvidia.\& Ns Ar N Ns .uuid
The GPU UUID, fetched at each read.
The read fails with
.Er EIO
until the GPU has been initialized, for example by opening
.Pa /dev/nvidia Ns Ar N
or by running
the nvidia-smi tool, where installed;
read it again afterwards.
.It Va dev.nvidia.\& Ns Ar N Ns .pci_bus_id
The PCI location as
.Qq DDDD:BB:SS.F ,
in lowercase hexadecimal.
The domain DDDD has at least four digits, zero padded, and more when the domain
is above 0xffff, for example
.Qq 10000:01:00.0 .
The bus BB and slot SS have two digits and the function F one digit.
.El
.Pp
The
.Nm nvidia-modeset
module provides
.Va hw.nvidiamodeset.version ,
its short version string; this module has no banner.
.Va hw.nvidiamodeset.version_string
equals it.
.Va hw.nvidiamodeset.version_major ,
.Va hw.nvidiamodeset.version_minor ,
.Va hw.nvidiamodeset.version_patch ,
.Va hw.nvidiamodeset.version_available
and
.Va hw.nvidiamodeset.version_suffix
have the same meanings and availability rules as the corresponding
.Va hw.nvidia.version
variables.
.Sh LOADER TUNABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.nvidia.registry.EnableGpuFirmware
0 turns the GSP firmware off, 1 turns it on, 2 (the default) applies the
driver's own policy.
These GPUs run only on the firmware, so 0 is not a useful setting, and the
installation instructions set 1.
The driver reads the
.Va hw.nvidia.registry.*
variables from the kernel environment when the module loads, so a change takes
effect after a reboot, or after
.Xr kldunload 8
and
.Xr kldload 8
of the driver.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Pa /dev/nvidiactl
.It Pa /dev/nvidia Ns Ar N
.It Pa /boot/modules/nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw.ko
.It Pa /boot/modules/nvidia_gsp_tu10x_fw.ko
(amd64 only)
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/devd/nvidia_gb10.conf
the
.Xr devd 8
rule that loads the driver
.It Pa /var/db/nvidia-gb10-freebsd/installed.manifest
every file
.Cm make install
wrote, with its mode, owner and sha256
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Dq KLD nvidia.ko: depends on nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw_fw - not available or version mismatch
.Xr kldload 8
of the driver found no firmware module of that name, and the driver is not
loaded.
The name is the module name: install the file
.Pa nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw.ko
(or
.Pa nvidia_gsp_tu10x_fw.ko ,
for the module named
.Dq nvidia_gsp_tu10x_fw_fw )
in the module directory, run
.Xr kldxref 8
on it, and load the driver again.
.It Dq nvidiaN: NVRM: rm_init_adapter() failed!
The first open of the GPU failed in the resource manager, and the open returns
.Er EIO .
The
.Dq RmInitAdapter failed!
message just before it gives the status.
Check with
.Xr kldstat 8
that the firmware modules are loaded, and that
.Va hw.nvidia.registry.EnableGpuFirmware
is not 0: if it is, correct
.Xr loader.conf 5
and reboot, or unload and load the driver, before opening again.
For any other cause the driver has freed the per-device stacks and released
its lock, so the open can simply be repeated.
.It Dq insufficient privileges to load firmware image
The opener could not load a firmware module, and the GPU needs one that is not
loaded: the driver was built without the module for that GPU, or it was not
installed with the driver.
Load
.Pa nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw.ko
(Turing and GA100: nvidia_gsp_tu10x_fw.ko) with
.Xr kldload 8
as root before the first open.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nvidia_drm_render 4 ,
.Xr nvidia_linux 4 ,
.Xr loader.conf 5 ,
.Xr kldload 8 ,
.Xr kldstat 8 ,
.Xr kldxref 8 ,
.Xr firmware 9
.Sh HISTORY
This is the CloudBSD port of NVIDIA's open
.Fx
